abstract: '<p>This record contains the hands-on materials for the SC26 tutorial &ldquo;Workload-Manager-Agnostic
  QPU Integration with QRMI&rdquo; (TUT-171), held on November 16, 2026 in Chicago.
  The tutorial shows how quantum processing units (QPUs) and quantum emulators can
  be brought into conventional HPC batch environments through QRMI (Quantum Resource
  Management Interface). QRMI is an open interface that exposes quantum resources
  and tasks through a common API, with an implementation specific to each provider.</p>

  <p>The materials put one integration pattern into practice on four HPC workload
  managers: Slurm, OpenPBS, IBM Spectrum LSF (Community Edition) and Open Cluster
  Scheduler (OCS). They use three quantum providers: IBM Quantum, Pasqal and Alice
  &amp; Bob. The pattern follows a batch job through its whole life. A job requests
  a named quantum resource. Validator plugins check the request when it is submitted,
  and the scheduler represents the resource as a license, counter or complex. A sensor
  can report the resource&rsquo;s availability. Job execution plugins (the Slurm SPANK
  plugin, OpenPBS hooks, the OCS prolog and epilog, the LSF JOB_STARTER) acquire the
  resource before the user program runs and release it afterwards.</p>

  <p>The repository contains:</p>

  <ul>

  <li><strong>containers/</strong>: Docker/Podman Compose definitions that build a
  small cluster on a laptop for each workload manager (one head node and two compute
  nodes). They include add-ons for the local IBM Quantum System API emulator and for
  a mock of Pasqal&rsquo;s Warden service, which simulates a QPU with QuTiP.</li>

  <li><strong>hands-on/</strong>: four tutorial modules.

  <ul>

  <li>Module 1 installs and configures the QRMI integration of the chosen scheduler.</li>

  <li>Module 2 submits backend-specific payloads through the low-level QRMI task API:
  a Qiskit Sampler input, a serialized Pulser sequence or a QIR program.</li>

  <li>Module 3 runs the same kind of programs with the providers&rsquo; SDKs (Qiskit
  Sampler, Pulser) through QRMI.</li>

  <li>The optional Module 4 passes quantum results to an MPI post-processing program
  run by the same workload manager.</li>

  <li>Also included: an exercise that observes how the scheduler holds, cancels and
  releases the resource, a reference for the QRMI configuration format, and a glossary
  of terms.</li>

  </ul>

  </li>

  <li><strong>onboarding/</strong>: account setup guides for IBM Quantum, Pasqal Cloud
  and Alice &amp; Bob.</li>

  </ul>

  <p>All exercises can run on emulators (IBM&rsquo;s local emulator, Pasqal&rsquo;s
  EMU_FREE and the local Warden mock, Alice &amp; Bob&rsquo;s Felis emulators), so
  a QPU account is not required. The materials have been tested with Docker, Docker
  Desktop, Rancher Desktop, Podman and WSL on Linux, macOS and Windows hosts, on x86_64
  and aarch64. They also include a checklist of questions for taking the pattern to
  production at a site: credentials, network access, accounting and failure recovery.</p>

  <p>IBM Spectrum LSF Community Edition is not redistributed in this record. It must
  be downloaded separately, as described in containers/lsf/docs/TUTORIAL_DOWNLOADS.md.</p>'
